################################################################################

How to manage ebUniversalProForEthernet-x86_64.ko

The following script is available to assist you loading and unloading the module:

    bash ebdriverlauncher.sh [start|stop|status]

'start' action
-----------------------
This script takes the current version of the module and loads it into 
memory: it starts the driver. It also creates all the expected device nodes and 
configures the network stack appropriately.

'stop' action
-----------------------
This script unloads the module from memory: it stops the driver.

'status' action
-----------------------
This scripts reports whether the kernel module is currently loaded in memory.

The target PC must have been compiled to allow loadable module.
    
This also involve that your target has the insmod and rmmod available..  

################################################################################

How to setup ebUniversalProForEthernet-x86_64.ko to load at boot time

NOTE: This script may be unavailable on some platforms that do not have the
requirements. On these platforms, simply build the driver with build.sh and
load it with ebdriverlaucher.sh.

The following script is available to assist you loading and unloading the module:

    bash install_driver.sh [--install|--uninstall]

--install action
-----------------------
Installs and activates the ebdriverlauncher.sh script in /etc/init.d so that 
the driver is automatically started at boot time. 

--uninstall
-----------------------
Removes the ebdriverlauncher.sh from /etc/init.d to stop the driver from
automatically be started at boot time.

Not providing any action starts the script in interactive mode. The user is
then guided through the process of either installing or uninstalling the
driver for automatic startup at boot time.

################################################################################

How to build ebUniversalProForEthernet-x86_64.ko

The eBUS Universal Pro module can be rebuilt by the customer to work on a different
kernel. It must also be rebuilt after a kernel update. 

The code is split into 2 main parts to allow an easy recompilation and distribution.

The first part is the libebTransportLayer-x86_64 static library that contains the logic of
the driver. This part is not refering to any file from the kernel and only depends
on itself to be built.

The second part is an abstraction layer that includes all the dependencies on the 
kernel header files. It can be subdivised into a bunch of wrappers, the main module
structure and a netfilter hook.

To build this module, you must first ensure that the kernel header files
you want to build against are installed on your computer. You can only compile the 
32 bits module against the 32 bits linux kernel and a 64 bits module against a 64
bits linux kernel.

Compiling the module:
    bash build.sh [--output=OUTPUT] [--kernel=KERNEL] [--help]
		where 
            --src=SRC_FOLDER is the location where the source code is located
			--output=OUTPUT is the location where the module folder
				will be created by the script. This folder will contain all
				that is needed for the distribution of the module. 
			--kernel=KERNEL points to the kernel header files. This optional argument
				is only	required when cross-compiling for another kernel that 
				is not the one actually in use by your computer
				i.e. /lib/modules/2.6.18-92.el5/build
            --cross_compile=PATH/PREFIX is a variable containing the full path to the prefix 
                compiler tool as the CROSS_COMPILE variable used by the
                linux kernel Makefiles  
                i.e. [some path on your hd]/bin/powerpc-e500v2-linux-gnuspe-
    		--help Display the help about the script build.sh

By default, the build can be done when installed in the default location by running the command : 
    ./build.sh
without parameters. This will create a the module for a host and for this host. 

################################################################################

How to build ebUniversalProForEthernet-x86_64.ko for a different kernel than the one currently used

If you have to compile the module for the current host but with a different kernel,
you can use the command : 
    ./build.sh --kernel=/lib/modules/[Kernel version]/build
This should build it without the need to reboot on the given kernel.

################################################################################

How to cross-compile ebUniversalProForEthernet-x86_64.ko

To cross-compile the module for a different target, you will have to provide
more information when calling the build script. Modify the command :
    ./build.sh --kernel=../../BuildRoot/buildroot-2013.02/output/target/lib/modules/3.8.4-rt2/build
       --cross_compile=../../x-tools/powerpc-e500v2-linux-gnuspe/bin/powerpc-e500v2-linux-gnuspe-
The --kernel will point to the build folder of the linux tree, the --cross_compile will contain
the full path and the prefix for the compilation tools.

################################################################################
  
Why do I get a tainted kernel message in the /var/log/messages when loading ebUniversalProForEthernet-x86_64.ko

A message looking like:
  [ 1307.978398] ebUniversalPro: module license 'ebUniversalPro-ppc' taints kernel.
can be added to /var/log/messages because the ebUniversalProForEthernet-x86_64.ko is not a GPL
module.

